## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/services/contentstack.ts** (`options`): This is where the Contentstack SDK Stack is configured. The `options` object passed to `contentstack.Stack()` lacks the `live_preview` property, so the Stack is initialized without live preview support — meaning ALL downstream content queries (getEntry, getSingleEntry via ContentstackFactory) can only return published content. The Contentstack JS SDK requires `live_preview` config at Stack initialization to enable draft/preview data retrieval. This is the CAUSE: every component that reads content (useContent → ContentstackContext → ContentstackProvider → defaultContent) ultimately gets data fetched through this Stack, which cannot return preview content because it was never configured to.
  - **Minimal fix:** Add `live_preview` configuration to the `options` object (lines 55–67), controlled by a new env var `CONTENTSTACK_LIVE_PREVIEW`. Before the closing `};` of the options object, add: `live_preview: CONTENTSTACK_LIVE_PREVIEW === 'true' ? { enable: true, host: CONTENTSTACK_LIVE_PREVIEW_HOST || 'https://live.contentstack.com' } : { enable: false },`. Also destructure `CONTENTSTACK_LIVE_PREVIEW` and `CONTENTSTACK_LIVE_PREVIEW_HOST` from `process.env` alongside the existing env vars (lines 17–24). This is the minimal change: the Contentstack SDK automatically handles preview token resolution and draft content delivery when `live_preview.enable` is true at Stack init time — no changes needed in getEntry, ContentstackFactory, or any consumer.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- Given a draft (unpublished) entry exists in the CMS for the GO Transit brand, when a content author opens the GO Transit preview page, then the page displays the draft headline and body text that exist only in that unpublished entry, not the currently published version.
- Given a draft (unpublished) entry exists in the CMS for the UP Express brand, when a content author opens the UP Express preview page, then the page displays the draft headline and body text from that unpublished UP Express entry.
- Given a draft (unpublished) entry exists in the CMS for the MX brand, when a content author opens the MX preview page, then the page displays the draft headline and body text from that unpublished MX entry.
- Given a content author is viewing a preview page and saves an edit to the entry in the CMS, when the save completes, then the updated text appears on the preview page without a full page reload.
- Given a content author is viewing draft content in preview mode, when the preview page renders, then a visible on-screen indicator (such as a preview or draft banner/badge) shows that the displayed content is unpublished draft content.
- Given an entry has both a published version and a newer unpublished draft in the CMS, when a visitor opens the live public page (not preview mode), then the page displays the published headline and body text, with no draft-only content visible.

